{
  "abstract": "A point of the square lattice has eight neighbours. Let delta(n) be the greatest upper density of a set S such that every point of S has at most n neighbours in S. Elkies introduced delta(n) and proved delta(3) = 1/2, which implies that no still Life of Conway's Game of Life has density greater than 1/2 (the still-Life conjecture). Ionascu, Pritikin and Wright studied the limit of the densities of n-dependent sets in finite kings graphs and conjectured the values 3/5 and 9/13 for n = 4 and n = 5, with upper bounds 0.608956 and 0.693943. This project proves delta(3) = 1/2, the same for still Lifes, delta(4) = 3/5 and delta(5) = 9/13, each as the greatest element of the set of upper densities. For n = 3 the proof is the one of Chu and Stuckey. For n = 4 and n = 5 the upper bound is an inequality in windows of four or three rows of five cells with two functions whose contributions telescope; the functions are given by tables, and the kernel checks the inequality in every window. Elkies reports that M. Moore announced delta(5) = 9/13 in 1995. We have not found a published proof for n = 4 or n = 5; whether these two results are new is unknown to us.",
